WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.347
CaMeL: control flow and data flow.

2
00:00:02.627 --> 00:00:14.552
Google DeepMind’s CaMeL (“Defeating Prompt Injections by Design,” Debenedetti and colleagues, 2025) builds on the dual LLM pattern and closes that hole.

3
00:00:14.832 --> 00:00:16.432
It makes two separations.

4
00:00:16.712 --> 00:00:19.635
Control flow comes only from the trusted query.

5
00:00:19.915 --> 00:00:26.251
The privileged model turns the user’s request into a small program in a restricted Python-like language.

6
00:00:26.531 --> 00:00:31.949
The program fixes which tools run and in what order before any untrusted data is read.

7
00:00:32.229 --> 00:00:40.677
Untrusted data, parsed by the quarantined model into structured values, can fill in arguments but cannot add, remove, or reorder steps.

8
00:00:40.957 --> 00:00:48.744
Data flow is tracked with capabilities: metadata attached to every value recording where it came from and who is allowed to read it.

9
00:00:49.024 --> 00:00:52.395
When values are combined, their capabilities combine too.

10
00:00:52.675 --> 00:00:59.032
Before each tool call, a custom interpreter checks the arguments’ capabilities against security policies.

11
00:00:59.312 --> 00:01:11.003
A policy for sending email might say: the recipient must not come from untrusted content, unless it is in the user’s contacts, and every recipient must be an allowed reader of the body.

12
00:01:11.283 --> 00:01:14.803
If a policy fails, the call is blocked or the user is asked.

13
00:01:15.083 --> 00:01:26.880
The paper reports that, on the AgentDojo benchmark, CaMeL solved 77% of tasks with provable security, compared with 84% for an undefended system.

14
00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:39.875
That gap is the honest price of the design, and the paper is open about others: someone has to write and maintain the policies, and asking users to confirm policy violations brings back the fatigue problem.

15
00:01:40.155 --> 00:01:50.181
Willison’s commentary adds that its strength is that it doesn’t rely on more AI to catch AI; the guarantees come from the interpreter, which is ordinary code.

16
00:01:50.461 --> 00:01:52.787
You’ll build a small version of that check below.
